WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Hi, everyone. Welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners Episode 43.

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:08.880
It's May 19th, 2026, some awesome AI developments today,

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.360
and we're going to make all of it make sense. Let's get into it.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.660
So imagine your smart speaker could do more than just answer one quick question at a time.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:22.040
What if it could turn into a full-production studio that researches a topic,

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:26.020
writes a script, and records an entire podcast episode for you?

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:30.100
That is exactly what Amazon just added to Alexa Plus.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:35.000
You tell it any subject you want, and the system builds a back-and-forth discussion

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.780
between two AI hosts. Complete with natural flow and realistic voices.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:45.360
Think of it like having a tiny radio station inside your echo device that plans the whole show

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:50.400
instead of just reading facts one by one. This goes beyond basic text to speech,

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:55.080
which simply reads words out loud. Because the AI now creates structure,

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:58.460
questions, and replies that connect like real conversation.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:03.560
Podcasts already help so many students and teens learn about history, science,

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:06.720
or even video game updates while walking or doing chores.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.700
Now you can create one instantly on any topic that interests you.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:14.700
For you personally, this could change how you study or explore hobbies.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:19.300
Because a quick audio summary might feel easier than reading another long article.

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:23.040
If you have an echo device with the Alexa Plus upgrade,

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:24.480
try saying something like,

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:28.840
create a podcast about how video games are made, and see what it produces.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:31.440
Okay, now from my favorite part of the show,

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:34.200
the Deep Dive, where we look under the hood.

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:39.860
Today's concept is how an AI turns a simple topic into a full scripted conversation.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.780
You already know how your phone sometimes suggests the next word while you text a friend.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:50.260
Now, picture that same idea, except the system guesses an entire back-and-forth exchange

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:52.700
between two people instead of just one word.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:57.500
First, it reads your topic and decides what the two hosts should cover in order,

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:01.060
like mapping out a short road trip with stops along the way.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:04.960
Next, it writes lines for each host that sound like real talk,

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:07.980
including questions and replies that connect naturally,

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:11.700
similar to how friends build on each other's ideas during a chat.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:17.460
Then, it picks different voices for each host and adds small pauses or tone changes

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:22.600
so nothing sounds robotic. The way a director might coach actors to pause for effect.

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:26.300
Finally, as stitches everything together into one audio file,

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:28.280
you can play like any other podcast,

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:32.140
and that is basically what Alexa is doing when it builds these shows.

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:37.540
It is using the same word prediction trick at a much larger scale to create the whole script,

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:40.540
and then turn that script into spoken audio.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:44.100
So next time someone mentions an AI-generated podcast,

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:50.060
you can tell them it is basically supercharged auto-complete that also knows how to talk to itself.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:51.480
Not so scary, right?

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:55.360
Now, let's look at some cool stuff you can actually try yourself.

00:02:56.060 --> 00:03:01.560
One user on Reddit shared a simple workflow that combines Claude's design tools with 11 labs

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:04.860
for voice to create short animated explainer videos.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:11.140
The trick is giving the AI both a script and timing information so the visuals line up with the

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:15.600
spoken words, like matching pictures to a storybook as you read it aloud.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:21.140
Anyone who likes making YouTube style videos for school projects should give this a go.

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:26.780
Head to Claude.ai, ask it to write a short script about something you are learning,

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:31.520
then use a free text-to-speech site like 11 labs to record the audio.

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:36.660
Feed the audio timestamps back into Claude to generate matching video scenes,

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:39.640
and you will have a polished explainer for under a dollar.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:45.520
Another thing worth checking out is Google's big annual IO keynote happening tomorrow.

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:50.660
It is expected to show off new AI features across Android and other products,

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:55.280
all presented in one live stream. You do not need any special setup,

00:03:55.500 --> 00:04:01.460
just a browser or phone. Go to the official Google IO website or YouTube channel tomorrow morning

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:06.980
and watch the live stream. Pause afterward and try any new tools they demonstrate right away,

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:11.900
turning the announcements into hands-on experiments. Time for a couple of quick bits.

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:17.600
A company called Auster released the world's first color LiDAR sensor for robots and cars.

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:23.480
This lets self-driving vehicles and robots see the world with both three-dimensional shape

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:28.820
and full color at the same time, more like human vision instead of flat point clouds.

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:33.880
And the Pope is preparing the first major statement on AI later this month.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:39.900
Pope LEO the 14 will present an encyclical focused on artificial intelligence,

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:43.140
with an anthropic co-founder invited to speak.

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:49.320
It is shaping up to be one of the highest profile ethical discussions about AI happening right now.

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:55.460
That's it for today. Remember every AI expert started exactly where you are right now.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:59.140
If something we talked about today made you curious, go try it.

